                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong large category
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            which is not (necessarily)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
          %p
            There are some variations in usage depending on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            chosen. Also, not all authors agree on whether a large category is
            %em not
            small, or merely not
            %em necessarily
            small (i.e., whether small categories are also large).
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            The precise meaning of the above definition depends on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            chosen.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZF"} ZF
                %em small category
                is one whose objects and morphisms form
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
                %em large category
                is one whose objects and morphisms form (perhaps proper)
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classes"} classes
            %li
              %p
                In ZF with a specified
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universe"} Grothendieck universe
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                %em small category
                is one whose objects and morphisms belong to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                (or have cardinalities belonging to
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                %em large category
                means a category whose objects and morphisms are still sets, but not (necessarily) belonging to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %li
              %p
                More generally, for any universe
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                as
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi U
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                    \-small
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi U
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                    \-large
                = succeed "-small" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                containing
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                = succeed "-large" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                with
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in V
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                may be called
                %strong very large
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "#Shulman08"}> Shulman (2008), p. 18
                \&amp;rbrack;.
          %p In all cases, it is somewhat ambiguous whether “large category” means “properly large,” i.e., large and not small, or whether small categories should be considered as a subclass of large categories. Usage may vary depending on need.
          %h3#largeness_and_moderateness Largeness and moderateness
          %p
            A
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moderate+category"} moderate category
            may be defined as one whose collections of objects and morphisms are no bigger than the size of the universe of small sets. This is related to largeness in different ways depending on the foundations.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In ZF, where all classes are subclasses of the class of all (small) sets, then
                %em all
                categories (small or large) are moderate.
            %li
              %p
                On the other hand, when smallness and largeness are defined with respect to a Grothendieck universe
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %li
              %p
                An in-between case, with some resulting ambiguity, is that of a class-set theory such as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NBG"} NBG
                or
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MK"} MK
                %em small category
                means one whose objects and morphisms form sets. Since such theories are closely related to ZF (indeed, NBG is conservative over ZF), it is natural to want to use “large category” to coincide with its ZF meaning: a category whose objects and morphisms form classes.
              %p However, in this case one can also consider categories which are “very large”: their objects and morphisms are “superclasses” or “conglomerates” (collections of classes defined by first-order formulas, just as we do for classes in ZF). Thus, in this case it may be more appropriate to say “moderate” for a category whose objects and morphisms are classes, and “large” for one which is not (necessarily) even moderate.
              %p
                This latter terminology also accords better with the usage for universes, since any Grothendieck universe
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                gives rise to a model of NBG (and even MK) whose sets are the sets in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                and whose classes are the subsets of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              %p
                On the other hand, one could import terminology the other way, and use “large category” even in the context of a universe
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                to mean one whose objects and morphisms are
                %em subsets
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                (i.e. what we have called above a “(
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                \-)moderate category”). This usage is that of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+Work"} Categories Work
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            Many tools and results about small categories, in particular concerning
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+categories"} functor categories
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Many large categories have all
                %em small
                limits and/or colimits, but a large category with
                %em large
                limits or colimits must (at least in
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
                %em some
                large limits and colimits, however, although they are not always very useful; see for instance
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+category"} total category
            %li
              %p
                In a foundation of ZF, NBG, or MK, one cannot form the functor category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C,D]
                unless
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is small. In a foundation with universes, one can form
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi C
                        %mo ,
                        %mi D
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C,D]
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                are.
          %p There are various notions and techniques to deal with this problem and reduce or relate large categories to small categories as much as possible:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In the simplest case the large category is
                %em essentially
                small in that it is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
                to a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            %li
              %p
                Many large categories that arise in practice are (even essentially) large but still
                = succeed "." do
                  %em accessible
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+category"} accessible category
                is a large category which behaves like the category of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ind-object"} ind-object
            %li
              %p
                Some problems can be avoided by simply assuming a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universe"} Grothendieck universe
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"}> small category
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} locally small category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moderate+category"} moderate category
            %li
              %p
                %strong large category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+enlargement"} universe enlargement
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/very+large+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaf+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} very large (∞,1)-sheaf (∞,1)-topos
          %h2#references References
          %p
            See most references on
